  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Tuesday)\n\nUpdate at 514 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026...\nTropical Storm Isaias has formed as of the 09Z update. A faster\nmovement and track through AL into middle TN is now forecast,\nwhich may increase the impactful gusty wind potential as the\npotential hurricane weakens and moves rapidly northward through\nthe TN Valley Saturday. We have adjusted PoPs to account for\nearlier arrival of outer bands of rainfall Friday night due to \nthe faster movement. The faster movement may lessen residence time\nof heavy rainfall and could keep the flood risk low. \nNevertheless, will continue to message a low risk of flooding due \nto tropical rainfall associated with the system that is expected \nand with the Slight Risk issued by WPC for Days 3-4.\n\nIssued at 906 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nAs noted above, Tropical Storm Isaias has formed in the Gulf and \nwill be the feature of focus that will potentially impact the \nTennessee Valley this weekend. This storm is officially forecast \nto strengthen into a hurricane as it approaches the north central \nGulf Coast late Friday. As it does so (and eventually comes ashore\nFriday night into Saturday morning), it will be interacting and \neventually merging with a shortwave trough pivoting into the lower\nMississippi Valley. This should accelerate the system during the \nday on Saturday as it moves northward from the Gulf Coast into the\nMid South/Tennessee Valley by the afternoon/evening. The tropical\nwind field with this system should weaken considerably into a \nDepression or lower by the time it moves this far north. However, \nthe strong pressure gradient will still allow for some potential \nAdivosry-level wind gusts of at least 30-40 MPH, especially along \nthe higher terrain. \n\nAdditionally, the track of this feature will be important. If it \nmakes landfall in the FL Panhandle and then swings north over our \neastern areas, this would place our area in the developing dry \nslot -- lowering rainfall amounts and keeping any stronger \nthunderstorm activity and tropical tornado threat to our south and\neast. However, it it were to make landfall further west, with the\nremnant core of the system remaining over eastern Mississippi, \nthis could put us in line for some slightly higher QPF and at \nleast a low tornado threat. Better resolution of the track will \ncome to light as the system forms and organizes over the next \n24-36 hours. In the meantime, residents of the Tennessee Valley \nshould be aware and prepared for a potentially windy/rainy \nSaturday into Saturday night and stay tuned and up to date on the\nlatest forecast information -- especially if they have travel \nplans this weekend. \n\nBy Sunday, the system will be departing into the Ohio Valley and \nCentral Appalachians. Breezy winds and low rain chances will \ndiminish by the evening hours on Sunday, with more tranquil \nweather on the horizon for early next week as high pressure builds\nin from the west.",
